# LeetCode 61 — Rotate List

## Problem Statement

Given the head of a linked list, rotate the list to the right by `k` places.

## LeetCode Question No.

61 — Rotate List

## Approach

1. Find the length of the linked list.
2. Reduce `k` using `k % size`.
3. If `k == 0`, return the original head.
4. Move to the node at position `size - k`.
5. Store the next node as the new head.
6. Disconnect the list at that position.
7. Connect the last node to the original head.
8. Return the new head.

## Time Complexity

**O(n)**

## Space Complexity

**O(1)**

## Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* rotateRight(ListNode* head, int k) {

        if (head == NULL || head->next == NULL) {
            return head;
        }

        ListNode* temp = head;
        int size = 0;
        int l = 1;

        while (temp != NULL) {
            size++;
            temp = temp->next;
        }

        k = k % size;

        if (k == 0) {
            return head;
        }

        temp = head;

        while (l < size - k) {
            temp = temp->next;
            l++;
        }

        ListNode* prev = temp;
        ListNode* new_head = temp->next;
        ListNode* a = temp->next;

        while (a->next != NULL) {
            a = a->next;
        }

        prev->next = NULL;
        a->next = head;

        head = new_head;

        return head;
    }
};
```

## Mistakes & Learning

* Initially handled `k % size` only when `k > size`. This fails when `k == size` or `k` is a multiple of `size`.
* Learned to normalize `k` using `k = k % size` directly.
* Learned to handle the `k == 0` case after normalization.
* Main linked-list rotation approach was correct.

## Optimization

The solution uses **O(1) extra space** and **O(n) time**.
